# Line endings.
#
# The repository has always stored LF (confirmed: `git ls-files --eol` reports
# `i/lf`).  What varies is the WORKING TREE: with core.autocrlf=true a Windows
# checkout gets CRLF, and that is a per-machine setting no one else inherits.
# This file makes the intent explicit and independent of local git config.
#
# WHY eol=lf MATTERS HERE, and it is not cosmetic.
# These scripts are executed by bash (msys2 on Windows, native elsewhere).
# Bash tolerates a stray CR on a simple command line but REJECTS it inside a
# compound statement -- if/for/while/case.  A CRLF working tree therefore
# breaks any script containing a loop or conditional, while leaving
# straight-line scripts working.  That is why the failure looks random.
#
# Observed 2026-08-25 on a Windows checkout: linux/aarch64/update.sh,
# compile/mnemonic.sh, windows/x64/gmp.sh and windows/x64/compile_app_debug.sh
# all fail `bash -n` purely from CR, and the Qt6 scripts did too until fixed.
#
# eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf per pattern, so the Windows working tree gets
# LF for these files and bash never sees a CR.  Modern Windows editors
# (VS Code, Notepad++, and Notepad since Win10 1809) all handle LF fine.

# Default: normalise text to LF in the repo, LF in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the file types that break, so intent survives any future edit
# to the default line above.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf

# Archives and images must never be touched.
*.tar.gz binary
*.tar.xz binary
*.zip    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
